    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I got a bit carried away with the goblin analogy. Let me refocus on the biology.\n\nThe lactobacillus and Saccharomyces cerevisiae in sourdough starter work together through a process called co-fermentation. The lactobacillus feeds on the sugars present in the dough, converting them into lactic acid through a series of metabolic reactions. This process is catalyzed by the enzyme lactate dehydrogenase, which is produced by the lactobacillus.\n\nMeanwhile, the yeast (Saccharomyces cerevisiae) consumes the sugars and produces carbon dioxide gas as a byproduct of glycolysis. This CO2 is trapped in the dough, causing it to rise. The yeast's activity is also influenced by the presence of lactic acid, which can inhibit its growth and activity.\n\nAs the lactobacillus continues to ferment the sugars, it produces more lactic acid, which in turn inhibits the yeast's growth and activity. This creates a delicate balance between the two microorganisms, with the lactobacillus dominating the fermentation process and producing the characteristic sour taste.\n\nThe interaction between the lactobacillus and yeast is a bit like a game of cat and mouse, with",
